{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"submission-repropose.json\", \"summary\": \"Turn 37 established the other half of the client-completion question: the protection that keeps an admitted operation alive when its instance is committed as NOOP in the owner-alive case, which also delimits the confirmed crash.\\n\\nWhat was observed locally. A harness admits one client operation with the real handlePropose for instance (0,0) — the record then has leader bookkeeping and one pending proposal — and then delivers a legal single-NONE Commit to the real handleCommit. The owner installs the instance as COMMITTED, clears the pending list and puts the operation back on the propose channel: pending_after 0, reproposed_on_channel 1. So in the owner-alive case the admitted operation is not dropped; it re-enters decision formation as a fresh proposal (which is the path that later produces the client's reply).\\n\\nNew obligation and check (claim C5-noop-commit-reproposes-admitted-operation, bounded liveness, priority 2). The duty is that a replica which owns an instance and still holds an admitted client operation for it must install a single-NONE commit and re-propose the operation onto its propose path rather than dropping it. Its scope is the owner-alive case, with the restarted-owner case (claim C4's crash), the client library's own retry and the later execution explicitly excluded; the binding locates handleCommit's NOOP re-proposal branch, and the plan asserts reproposed_on_channel == 1 on the commit event with the admitted-proposal prerequisite (bookkeeping present, one pending proposal) as admission alias and instance identity correlation. The local run being satisfied means the formal outcome is expected to hold, which is exactly the kind of bounded negative result that delimits the crash: the same handler protects the operation when bookkeeping exists and terminates the replica when it does not.\\n\\nFraming and